This product is a derivative of the author's local paper-fetch-mcp 0.1.0 project. That
local source did not contain a public licence; the author publishes this fork under the
MIT licence in LICENSE.

Changes in this fork, relative to upstream:

  * Added src/paper_fetch_mcp_safe/compliance.py: a pure, testable licence and
    source policy. Every PDF candidate is evaluated before download and carries a
    recorded decision (licence, normalised licence, licence class, evidence type,
    policy version) into the JSON sidecar.
  * Added the LICENSE_RESTRICTED error code. Refused candidates are reported as
    such instead of being reported as "no open-access copy".
  * Changed the default of allow_scihub from true to false. Gray sources are
    reachable only by explicit opt-in, which logs a startup warning.
  * Added license_policy with three levels: strict, open (default) and any.
  * Enforced the policy in the router, in the resolve/download workflow, and in
    HttpClient.download_pdf, so no download path can skip it.
  * Added licence information to get_server_status, to minimal resolve output,
    to download results, and to the batch summary (license_restricted count).
  * Renamed the distribution to paper-fetch-mcp-safe, the import package to
    paper_fetch_mcp_safe, the console script to paper-fetch-mcp-safe, the
    FastMCP server name, the User-Agent string and the PMC id-converter tool id,
    so this fork and upstream can be installed side by side.
  * Removed upstream's docs/ research notes, .tmp/ benchmark artifacts and the
    mirror-specific download run logs. Rewrote the live test script's gray stage
    as an offline audit stage that asserts the boundary holds.

Third-party services used at runtime (OpenAlex, Crossref, Unpaywall, Semantic
Scholar, CORE, Europe PMC, PMC, arXiv, HAL, Zenodo, OSF, bioRxiv/medRxiv,
OpenReview, ACL Anthology, PMLR) are governed by their own terms of use. This
project only reads their public APIs and the files those APIs designate as open.
